        "Response": {
          "message_id": "69d41406-3f57-41da-b4c4-b1e4c4bb13ed",
          "content": "A few ways:\n\n**On a pulled image locally:**\n```bash\ndocker inspect <image> --format '{{.Created}}'\n```\n\n**From a running pod in K8s:**\n```bash\nkubectl exec -n portfolio <pod> -- cat /proc/1/environ | tr '\\0' '\\n' | grep -i build\n```\n(only works if the build date was baked in as an env var)\n\n**From the registry without pulling:**\n```bash\ndocker manifest inspect <image> | grep created\n```\n\nOr via Docker Hub API:\n```bash\ncurl -s \"https://hub.docker.com/v2/repositories/<user>/<repo>/tags/<tag>\" | jq '.last_updated'\n```\n\nThe most reliable approach for your own images is to bake the build timestamp in at build time using a Docker label:\n\n```dockerfile\nARG BUILD_DATE\nLABEL org.opencontainers.image.created=$BUILD_DATE\n```\n\nThen pass it during build:\n```bash\ndocker build --build-arg BUILD_DATE=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ) ...\n```\n\nAnd inspect it later with:\n```bash\ndocker inspect <image> --format '{{index .Config.Labels \"org.opencontainers.image.created\"}}'\n```"
        }
