          "content": "Found it. The root cause is clear:\n\n**`WebClientConfig` registers two beans with the same name `\"loadBalancedWebClientBuilder\"`**, and `UserClient` always injects `WebClient.Builder loadBalancedWebClientBuilder` by name. In K8s, `application-k8s.yml` sets `load-balanced: false`, which means:\n- The `@LoadBalanced` bean is **not created** (condition false)\n- The `standardWebClientBuilder` bean **is created** under the name `\"loadBalancedWebClientBuilder\"`\n\nSo the injection in `UserClient` should actually get the plain builder. That part is correct.\n\nThe real problem is **`spring.cloud.loadbalancer.enabled: false` doesn't fully disable the load balancer interceptor** when the `@LoadBalanced` bean was already processed. But more importantly — looking at the log:\n\n```\n503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE from GET http://user-manager:8080/users\n```\n\nThe request **is reaching** `http://user-manager:8080` (K8s DNS), but getting a `503` back. This is **not a load balancer resolution failure** — that's a separate earlier warning from a different code path. The actual call is going through fine.\n\nThe `503` from `user-manager` is the real issue. Check:\n\n```bash\nkubectl get pods -n portfolio | grep user-manager\nkubectl logs -n portfolio deployment/user-manager --tail=50\n```\n\nMost likely `user-manager` is either not ready, crashing, or its readiness probe is failing. The load balancer warnings are a red herring from Spring Cloud's internal retry mechanism before it falls back to direct DNS."
