    "Good picture now. Here's my assessment:\n\n---\n\n## What's Done Well\n\n- `enabled` guards on every resource — clean feature-flag pattern\n- Separation of ConfigMaps (non-sensitive) and Secrets (sensitive) — correct\n- Named ports (`http-api`, `management`) used consistently in probes and services\n- Liveness/readiness probes on all app deployments hitting the management port\n- `helm.sh/hook` annotations on the migrations Job — correct lifecycle placement\n- `enableServiceLinks: false` on Neo4j with an explanatory comment — good\n- `b64enc` on all Secret values — correct\n- `initContainer` on analytics-manager to wait for Neo4j\n\n---\n\n## Issues & Gaps\n\n**1. No resource requests/limits on any container**\n\nEvery container is missing `resources`. Without them, pods can consume unbounded CPU/memory and the scheduler can't make good placement decisions.\n\n```yaml\nresources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: \"100m\"\n    memory: \"256Mi\"\n  limits:\n    memory: \"512Mi\"\n```\n\n**2. Infrastructure deployments have no probes**\n\nPostgres, RabbitMQ, Redis, Neo4j, and pgAdmin have no liveness/readiness probes. A pod that starts but whose process crashes silently will keep receiving traffic.\n\n**3. Infrastructure has no persistent volumes**\n\nPostgres, RabbitMQ, Redis, and Neo4j all use `Deployment` with no `PersistentVolumeClaim`. Data is lost on pod restart. For a portfolio/demo context this may be acceptable, but it should be a conscious decision — worth a comment at minimum.\n\n**4. `secrets.yaml` is a plain file in the repo**\n\n`secrets.yaml` contains real passwords in plaintext and is committed alongside the chart. This is the most significant issue. Standard approaches: use `helm-secrets` (SOPS-encrypted), or supply values at deploy time with `-f secrets.yaml` from outside the repo (and gitignore the file). At minimum it should be in `.gitignore`.\n\n**5. Inconsistent labels**\n\nSome resources are missing labels. For example, the postgres `Service`, rabbitmq `Service`, redis `Service`, neo4j `Service`, and postgres-migrations `Job` have no `labels` on their `metadata`. The postgres `Deployment` also has no `app.kubernetes.io/` recommended labels anywhere. This makes `kubectl` filtering and tooling harder.\n\n**6. `imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent` on app images with `latest`-style tags**\n\nSeveral images use `latest` (e.g. `rjosipovic/portfolio-common-notification-manager:latest`) but `IfNotPresent` means a newer `latest` will never be pulled on an existing node. Either use immutable tags (preferred) or switch to `Always` for `latest`-tagged images.\n\n**7. `challenge-manager-config` is missing `SERVICE_PORT` / `MANAGEMENT_PORT`**\n\n`user-manager-config` sets these but `challenge-manager-config` and `gamification-manager-config` don't. If those services read those env vars (which they likely do given the shared pattern), they'll fall back to hardcoded defaults silently.\n\n**8. `rewrite-target: /` on the Ingress is too broad**\n\n`nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /` rewrites every path to `/`, which breaks any path-based routing. Since both rules already use `path: /`, the annotation is redundant and potentially harmful if you ever add sub-path routes.\n\n**9. No `helm.sh/hook-delete-policy` for failed migrations**\n\nThe migrations Job has `hook-delete-policy: hook-succeeded` but not `hook-failed`. A failed job will persist indefinitely, blocking future upgrades because Helm won't re-create a hook resource that already exists.\n\n```yaml\n\"helm.sh/hook-delete-policy\": hook-succeeded,hook-failed\n```\n\n**10. `restartPolicy: OnFailure` on the migrations Job with no `backoffLimit`**\n\nWithout `backoffLimit`, Kubernetes defaults to 6 retries. A bad migration could run 6 times against the database. Set `backoffLimit: 2` or `0` depending on whether your migrations are idempotent.\n[Tool uses: none]",
