    "The right approach for a Helm chart is to define resources in `values.yaml` and reference them in templates — not hardcode them. That way they're easy to tune per environment.\n\nHere's the plan:\n\n1. Add a `resources` block to each service in `values.yaml` (apps + infrastructure)\n2. Add `resources: {{ toYaml .Values.xxx.resources | nindent 12 }}` to each deployment template\n\nLet me first check what containers we have to size appropriately:\n[Tool uses: fs_read]",
    "Here are the suggested resource values. Spring Boot JVMs need at least 256Mi to start reliably; infrastructure components vary:\n\n**In `values.yaml`**, add a `resources` block to each entry. Here's what to add for each section:\n\n**Apps** (Spring Boot services — all similar):\n```yaml\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        cpu: \"100m\"\n        memory: \"256Mi\"\n      limits:\n        memory: \"512Mi\"\n```\nApply this to: `frontend` (except use `64Mi`/`128Mi` — it's nginx), `notificationManager`, `userManager`, `apiGateway`, `challengeManager`, `gamificationManager`, `analyticsManager`.\n\n**Infrastructure:**\n```yaml\n# postgres\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        cpu: \"100m\"\n        memory: \"128Mi\"\n      limits:\n        memory: \"256Mi\"\n\n# rabbitmq\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        cpu: \"100m\"\n        memory: \"128Mi\"\n      limits:\n        memory: \"256Mi\"\n\n# redis\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        cpu: \"50m\"\n        memory: \"64Mi\"\n      limits:\n        memory: \"128Mi\"\n\n# neo4jAnalytics  (Neo4j is memory-hungry)\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        cpu: \"200m\"\n        memory: \"512Mi\"\n      limits:\n        memory: \"1Gi\"\n\n# pgadmin\n    resources:\n      requests:\n        cpu: \"50m\"\n        memory: \"128Mi\"\n      limits:\n        memory: \"256Mi\"\n```\n\nThen in each deployment template, add this line inside the container spec (same indentation level as `image:`):\n\n```yaml\n          resources: {{- toYaml .Values.apps.challengeManager.resources | nindent 12 }}\n```\n\nAdjust the `.Values` path per template:\n\n| Template | Values path |\n|---|---|\n| `challenge-manager.yaml` | `.Values.apps.challengeManager.resources` |\n| `user-manager.yaml` | `.Values.apps.userManager.resources` |\n| `gamification-manager.yaml` | `.Values.apps.gamificationManager.resources` |\n| `analytics-manager.yaml` | `.Values.apps.analyticsManager.resources` |\n| `notification-manager.yaml` | `.Values.apps.notificationManager.resources` |\n| `api-gateway.yaml` | `.Values.apps.apiGateway.resources` |\n| `frontend.yaml` | `.Values.apps.frontend.resources` |\n| `postgres.yaml` | `.Values.infrastructure.postgres.resources` |\n| `rabbitmq.yaml` | `.Values.infrastructure.rabbitmq.resources` |\n| `redis.yaml` | `.Values.infrastructure.redis.resources` |\n| `neo4j-analytics.yaml` | `.Values.infrastructure.neo4jAnalytics.resources` |\n| `pgadmin.yaml` | `.Values.infrastructure.pgadmin.resources` |\n\nNote: no CPU limit intentionally — CPU limits cause throttling even when the node has spare capacity, which is generally worse than no limit. Memory limits are set because OOM is a hard failure that needs a ceiling.\n\nGo ahead and apply, then I'll review.\n[Tool uses: none]",
