    "Yes, it's an improvement for consistency and maintainability. Named ports make the probes and services self-documenting and mean port numbers are defined in one place.\n\nHowever, the value depends on whether the infrastructure ports are likely to change. For something like postgres (`5432`) or redis (`6379`) — they're effectively constants and hardcoding them is common practice even in production charts. The real benefit of named ports + values is for app services where you control the port.\n\nMy take: it's worth doing for **rabbitmq** (two ports, both referenced in the service) and **neo4j** (two ports), but for postgres, redis, and pgadmin the gain is minimal since those ports never change and aren't referenced in probes by port name.\n\nThat said, if you want strict consistency across the chart, it's a clean improvement. Want to add it to the task list as a Low priority item?\n[Tool uses: none]",
