    "Two changes needed in `postgres-migrations.yaml`:\n\n**1. Add `hook-failed` to the delete policy** so a failed job doesn't block future upgrades:\n```yaml\n    \"helm.sh/hook-delete-policy\": hook-succeeded,hook-failed\n```\n\n**2. Add `backoffLimit`** — since migrations should be idempotent (Liquibase tracks applied changesets), 2 retries is reasonable:\n```yaml\nspec:\n  backoffLimit: 2\n  template:\n```\n\nBoth changes are in the `Job` spec. Go ahead and apply.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> Explain backofflimit.",
    "`backoffLimit` defines how many times Kubernetes will retry a failed Job pod before marking the Job as failed.\n\nWithout it, the default is **6 retries**. So if your migration fails (bad SQL, wrong credentials, etc.), Kubernetes will run the migration container 6 more times before giving up — potentially running partial migrations multiple times against your database.\n\nWith `backoffLimit: 2`, it retries twice then stops. Combined with Liquibase's changeset tracking, retries are safe — already-applied changesets are skipped. But you don't want unlimited retries if something is fundamentally broken.\n\n`restartPolicy: OnFailure` (which you already have) controls what happens at the pod level — the container restarts within the same pod. `backoffLimit` controls how many times a new pod is created after the previous one fails entirely.\n[Tool uses: none]",
