    "> Yes, we should implement PersistentVolumeClaims.",
    "Four services need PVCs: **postgres**, **rabbitmq**, **redis**, and **neo4j**.\n\nThe pattern for each is:\n1. Add a `PersistentVolumeClaim` resource to the template\n2. Mount it in the container at the data directory\n3. Add PVC size to `values.yaml`\n\nHere's what to add for each:\n\n---\n\n**`values.yaml`** — add `storage` under each infrastructure entry:\n\n```yaml\n  postgres:\n    storage: \"1Gi\"\n\n  rabbitmq:\n    storage: \"512Mi\"\n\n  redis:\n    storage: \"512Mi\"\n\n  neo4jAnalytics:\n    storage: \"2Gi\"\n```\n\n---\n\n**`postgres.yaml`** — append PVC and add volume mount:\n\n```yaml\n# Add to container spec:\n          volumeMounts:\n            - name: data\n              mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data\n      volumes:\n        - name: data\n          persistentVolumeClaim:\n            claimName: postgres-pvc\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: postgres-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes: [\"ReadWriteOnce\"]\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: {{ .Values.infrastructure.postgres.storage }}\n```\n\n---\n\n**`rabbitmq.yaml`**:\n```yaml\n          volumeMounts:\n            - name: data\n              mountPath: /var/lib/rabbitmq\n      volumes:\n        - name: data\n          persistentVolumeClaim:\n            claimName: rabbitmq-pvc\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: rabbitmq-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes: [\"ReadWriteOnce\"]\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: {{ .Values.infrastructure.rabbitmq.storage }}\n```\n\n---\n\n**`redis.yaml`**:\n```yaml\n          volumeMounts:\n            - name: data\n              mountPath: /data\n      volumes:\n        - name: data\n          persistentVolumeClaim:\n            claimName: redis-pvc\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: redis-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes: [\"ReadWriteOnce\"]\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: {{ .Values.infrastructure.redis.storage }}\n```\n\n---\n\n**`neo4j-analytics.yaml`**:\n```yaml\n          volumeMounts:\n            - name: data\n              mountPath: /data\n      volumes:\n        - name: data\n          persistentVolumeClaim:\n            claimName: neo4j-analytics-pvc\n---\napiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolumeClaim\nmetadata:\n  name: neo4j-analytics-pvc\nspec:\n  accessModes: [\"ReadWriteOnce\"]\n  resources:\n    requests:\n      storage: {{ .Values.infrastructure.neo4jAnalytics.storage }}\n```\n\nNote: `volumeMounts` goes inside the container spec, `volumes` goes at the pod spec level (same level as `containers`). Go ahead and apply, then I'll review.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> Made the changes, please review. Since we did not specify storage class, default one will be used and PV created by minikube. Where exactly on minicube host will these mount points exists?",
    "Let me review the changes first:\n[Tool uses: fs_read]",
    "There's a bug in rabbitmq, redis, and neo4j PVCs. The `storage` value in `values.yaml` is a plain string (`\"512Mi\"`), but you used `toYaml` on it which will serialize it incorrectly. Postgres is correct — use the same pattern for the other three:\n\n**rabbitmq.yaml** — change:\n```yaml\n    requests: {{- toYaml .Values.infrastructure.rabbitmq.storage | nindent 12 }}\n```\nto:\n```yaml\n    requests:\n      storage: {{ .Values.infrastructure.rabbitmq.storage }}\n```\n\nSame fix for **redis.yaml** and **neo4j-analytics.yaml**.\n\n---\n\nRegarding your question about where minikube stores the data — minikube's default `standard` StorageClass uses the `hostpath` provisioner, which creates PVs at:\n\n```\n/tmp/hostpath-provisioner/<namespace>/<pvc-name>/\n```\n\ninside the minikube VM. You can verify a specific PV's path with:\n\n```bash\nkubectl get pv -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{\"\\t\"}{.spec.hostPath.path}{\"\\n\"}{end}'\n```\n[Tool uses: none]",
