    "Found the root cause. The `UserClient` always uses `loadBalancedWebClientBuilder` (injected by Spring Cloud), which requires a discovery client to resolve `user-manager`. In K8s, load balancing is disabled via `spring.cloud.loadbalancer.enabled: false`, but the bean injected is still the load-balanced builder — it just has no instances registered, hence the error.\n\nThe fix is to conditionally use a plain `WebClient.builder()` when `load-balanced: false`. You already have `app.clients.user-manager.load-balanced` in `application-k8s.yml`, so wire it up:\n\n**`UserClient.java`** — change the constructor:\n\n```java\npublic UserClient(UserManagerClientConfig userManagerClientConfig, WebClient.Builder loadBalancedWebClientBuilder) {\n    this.userManagerClientConfig = userManagerClientConfig;\n    var baseUrl = userManagerClientConfig.getUsersEndpoint();\n    var builder = userManagerClientConfig.isLoadBalanced()\n            ? loadBalancedWebClientBuilder\n            : WebClient.builder();\n    this.webClient = builder\n            .baseUrl(baseUrl)\n            .filter(logRequest())\n            .filter(logResponse())\n            .build();\n}\n```\n\nAnd add `load-balanced` to `UserManagerClientConfig`:\n\n```java\nprivate boolean loadBalanced = true; // default true for docker/local\n```\n\n**`application.yml`** — add the default:\n```yaml\napp:\n  clients:\n    user-manager:\n      users-endpoint: ${USER_MANAGER_ENDPOINT:http://user-manager:8080/users}\n      load-balanced: true\n```\n\n`application-k8s.yml` already has `load-balanced: false`, so no change needed there.\n\n---\n\n**2. `503 Service Unavailable` (after the load balancer issue is fixed)**\n\nThe `503` comes from `http://user-manager:8080/users` — this is the second error, which happens after retries. Once the load balancer issue is fixed, this may resolve itself. But if it persists, check that `user-manager` is actually up and its readiness probe is passing:\n\n```bash\nkubectl get pods -n port