    "The user-manager logs tell the whole story:\n\n```\nGET /users?ids=55623fac-d880-44d1-ba9d-1e8af3a63ba7 → 200, body=[]\n```\n\nThe request **is reaching user-manager successfully** — no 503, no error. It returns an empty array because the user ID `55623fac...` doesn't exist in the database. The logged-in user is `028adc48...` (roman), but gamification-manager is querying for a different ID.\n\nThis means the leaderboard contains a user ID that was created in a previous deployment when the database was ephemeral (no PVC). After the PVC work we just did, postgres got a fresh volume and the user was re-registered with a new UUID — but the **gamification-manager's leaderboard data in Redis still references the old UUID**.\n\nThe fix is simply to flush the stale Redis data:\n\n```bash\nkubectl exec -n <namespace> deployment/redis -- redis-cli -u \"redis://<user>:<password>@localhost:6379\" FLUSHALL\n```\n\nOr more surgically, just delete the leaderboard key:\n\n```bash\nkubectl exec deployment/redis -- redis-cli -u \"redis://<user>:<password>@localhost:6379\" DEL leaderboard\n```\n\nAfter that, new leaderboard entries will use the current user IDs and the alias lookup will work correctly.\n[Tool uses: none]",
