    "Fair point. Let me look at the actual failing request from the original logs more carefully:\n\n```\n503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE from GET http://user-manager:8080/users\n```\n\nAnd the load balancer warnings:\n```\nNo servers available for service: user-manager\nLoadBalancer does not contain an instance for the service user-manager\n```\n\nThese warnings come from `ReactorLoadBalancerExchangeFilterFunction` — meaning the `@LoadBalanced` `WebClient.Builder` **is** being used despite `load-balanced: false` in `application-k8s.yml`.\n\nLet me check the `WebClientConfig` again more carefully:\n\nThe issue is that **two beans with the same name `\"loadBalancedWebClientBuilder\"` are declared**. Spring doesn't allow duplicate bean names — the second definition (`standardWebClientBuilder`) silently overrides the first. But the `@LoadBalanced` annotation on the first bean registers a `LoadBalancerInterceptor` with Spring Cloud **before** the override happens, and that interceptor gets attached to the builder.\n\nActually the more likely issue: `@ConditionalOnProperty` with `havingValue = \"false\"` — Spring evaluates this as a string comparison. If the property isn't being picked up correctly (e.g. profile not active), both conditions could fail and **neither bean is created**, causing Spring to fall back to an auto-configured load-balanced builder.\n\nVerify the active profile in the gamification-manager pod:\n\n```bash\nkubectl exec deployment/gamification-manager -- env | grep SPRING_PROFILES\n```\n\nIf `k8s` profile isn't active, `load-balanced: false` never gets set and the load balancer is used with no discovery backend — exactly matching the error.\n[Tool uses: none]",
