    "> Working with minikube to get fast feedback is becomming challenging. I was making changes to the code, building images, and deploying to minikube not knowing that minikube has its own cache of docker images. My workaround for gamification manager was to add pull policy always. Can you recommend more optimal procedure?",
    "The core issue is that minikube has its own Docker daemon separate from your host. Two better approaches:\n\n**Option 1: Build directly into minikube's Docker daemon (simplest)**\n```bash\neval $(minikube docker-env)\ndocker build -t rjosipovic/portfolio-math-challenges-gamification-manager:latest .\n# now the image is already inside minikube, no push/pull needed\n```\nKeep `imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent` — minikube finds the image locally and never tries to pull.\n\nRun `eval $(minikube docker-env --unset)` to restore your host Docker when done.\n\n**Option 2: Use minikube image load (no daemon switch needed)**\n```bash\ndocker build -t rjosipovic/...:latest .\nminikube image load rjosipovic/...:latest\n```\nSame result — image lands in minikube's cache. Slightly slower than option 1 but you stay on your host daemon.\n\n---\n\n**Recommended workflow for fast iteration:**\n\n1. `eval $(minikube docker-env)` once per terminal session\n2. Build the image\n3. `kubectl rollout restart deployment/gamification-manager`\n4. No registry push, no `imagePullPolicy: Always`, no cache surprises\n\nOption 1 is the standard minikube dev loop and gives the fastest feedback.\n[Tool uses: none]",
