    "> Explain imagePullPolicy: Always for latest-tagged images",
    "When a pod starts, Kubernetes decides whether to pull the image based on `imagePullPolicy`:\n\n- `IfNotPresent` — only pull if the image isn't already cached on the node\n- `Always` — always contact the registry to check for a newer digest, pull if different\n\nThe problem with `latest` tag + `IfNotPresent`: if minikube already has `rjosipovic/...:latest` cached from a previous deployment, it will **never pull the updated image** even after you push a new one to the registry. The tag hasn't changed, so Kubernetes thinks it already has it.\n\nWith `Always`, Kubernetes checks the registry on every pod start and pulls if the digest has changed.\n\n---\n\nHowever — this only matters when pulling from a **remote registry**. For your local minikube dev workflow (building directly into minikube's daemon with `eval $(minikube docker-env)`), `IfNotPresent` is actually correct since there's no registry involved at all.\n\nSo the practical recommendation is:\n- **Dev (minikube, local build)**: `IfNotPresent`\n- **CI/CD or any remote registry with `latest` tag**: `Always`\n\nSince all your images are tagged `latest` and you're deploying from a registry, `Always` is the safer default for the Helm chart. Ready to apply it?\n[Tool uses: none]",
